\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 The Background Noise Rule:}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 
 Your brain treats unmoving peripheral data exactly like the feeling of the clothes on your body or the smell of your own house. It classifies it as "useless background static" and simply stops processing it to save energy.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 The Peripheral Weakness:}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029  
Because your peripheral vision has a very low density of receptors compared to the center of your eye (the fovea), it is incredibly easy for your brain to "override" the edges of your vision and fill them in with whatever color is dominant in the center.

