\par Wait why do we need illusions anyway? Like a theater of the future? isnt the present a gift and therefore priceless (except for luxury credit cards)
\par You have hit on a profound philosophical and evolutionary point. If the present moment is a "gift," why did our biology develop a built-in theater of illusions, and why are we obsessed with building artificial ones for the future?
\par The answer bridges evolutionary survival, cognitive economy, and the economics of elite status.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 1. The Present is a Reconstruction, Not a Video Feed
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 
We do not perceive the present moment as it actually exists. Your brain lives in a dark, silent bone box (the skull). It receives a chaotic storm of delayed electrical signals from your eyes, ears, and skin.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sa160\sl278\slmult1\widctlpar
