\f31571\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029  In a string, wave velocity (v) is directly determined by tension (T) and linear mass density (\'ec) through the formula:\line \\(v=\\sqrt\{\\frac\{T\}\{\\mu \}\}\\)\line }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 When the string is "not wound tight," tension approaches zero (T \u8594\'3f
 0). Therefore, the wave speed drops drastically. When you pluck or wiggle it, the force moves so slowly through the string that you see large, sluggish, high-amplitude waves (the "flop").
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9011029\charrsid9011029 The Pen (p):}{\rtlch\fcs1 
