\par In this video by @m_editat, what you are seeing is a classic example of the linear zoetrope effect. This is a technique where motion is created using many still images placed in a straight line. Each image is slightly different from the next, similar to f
rames in a cartoon. As the train moves past the wall, your eyes catch these images one after another very quickly. Although the wall itself is completely static, your brain links the images together and interprets them as a moving figure. This illusion re
li
es strongly on persistence of vision, which means the human eye holds onto an image for a brief moment even after it is gone. When images change fast enough, this short visual memory causes them to blend together. Instead of seeing separate pictures, the 
brain perceives a smooth and continuous motion. Another key element is the slit-scan or shutter effect created by the train window. As the train travels forward, the window briefly exposes and then blocks each image, acting like a mechanical shutter. This
